[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Angular Directive Metadata: A Comprehensive Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Directives are a cornerstone of Angular, allowing you to extend HTML's capabilities and create reusable UI components. Understanding directive metadata is crucial for building robust and maintainable Angular applications. This tutorial will explore the key metadata properties: `selector`, `inputs`, `outputs`, `hostListeners`, and `hostBindings`."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Selector: Defining the Directive's Trigger"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `selector` property specifies how the directive is activated in the template. It can be an element name, attribute name, class name, or any combination thereof."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]'
})
export class HighlightDirective {
  // Directive logic here
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `HighlightDirective` is applied to any element with the `appHighlight` attribute.  You would use it in your template like this: `<p appHighlight>This text will be highlighted.</p>`"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Element selector: `selector: 'my-directive'` (matches `<my-directive>`)"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Attribute selector: `selector: '[myDirective]'` (matches `<div myDirective>`)"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Class selector: `selector: '.my-directive'` (matches `<div class="my-directive">`)"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Inputs: Passing Data into the Directive"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `inputs` property defines the data that can be passed into the directive from the parent component.  This allows for dynamic configuration of the directive's behavior."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]'
  inputs: ['highlightColor: color']
})
export class HighlightDirective {
  @Input('color') highlightColor: string;

  // Directive logic using highlightColor
}"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `highlightColor` is an input property. The template would pass the color like this: `<p appHighlight color="'red'">Highlighted text</p>`. The `highlightColor: color` syntax allows you to alias the input property name. In this case, the directive's property `highlightColor` is bound to the input `color` in the template."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Outputs: Emitting Events from the Directive"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `outputs` property defines the events that the directive can emit to the parent component. This enables the directive to communicate changes or actions back to the component that uses it."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"import { EventEmitter } from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[appClickTracker]'
  outputs: ['clickEvent: clicked']
})
export class ClickTrackerDirective {
  @Output('clicked') clickEvent = new EventEmitter<MouseEvent>();

  @HostListener('click', ['$event'])
  onClick(event: MouseEvent) {
    this.clickEvent.emit(event);
  }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `ClickTrackerDirective` emits a `clickEvent` (aliased as `clicked` in the template) whenever the host element is clicked. The parent component can then listen for this event: `<div appClickTracker (clicked)="handleClick($event)">Click me</div>`."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"4. HostListeners: Listening to Host Element Events"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `hostListeners` property allows you to listen for events on the host element (the element the directive is attached to) and execute a method in the directive when the event occurs."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]'
})
export class HighlightDirective {
  @HostListener('mouseenter') onMouseEnter() {
    // Logic to highlight the element on mouseenter
  }

  @HostListener('mouseleave') onMouseLeave() {
    // Logic to remove highlight on mouseleave
  }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"This directive listens for `mouseenter` and `mouseleave` events on the host element. When the mouse enters the element, the `onMouseEnter` method is executed, and when the mouse leaves, the `onMouseLeave` method is executed."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"5. HostBindings: Binding to Host Element Properties"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `hostBindings` property allows you to bind properties of the host element to properties of the directive. This enables you to dynamically control the host element's attributes, styles, and properties."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]'
  host: {
    '[style.backgroundColor]': 'backgroundColor'
  }
})
export class HighlightDirective {
  backgroundColor: string = 'yellow';
}"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example binds the `backgroundColor` property of the directive to the `style.backgroundColor` property of the host element.  The host element's background color will be set to 'yellow' initially. You can also use `@HostBinding` decorator instead of `host` metadata property."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"import { HostBinding } from '@angular/core';

@Directive({
  selector: '[appHighlight]'
})
export class HighlightDirective {
  @HostBinding('style.backgroundColor') backgroundColor: string = 'yellow';
}"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"This achieves the same result as the previous example, but uses the `@HostBinding` decorator for a more concise syntax."},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering these directive metadata properties, you can create powerful and reusable directives that enhance your Angular applications."}]
